  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 301 AM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nGLobal deterministic guidance continues to show a short wave trough \nmoving across the Rockies into the Great Plains Wednesday into \nWednesday night.  The wave moves northeast across the Upper Midwest \nThursday and the surface low pushes a cold front into Iowa.  The \nwave continues northeast away from the Mississippi Valley, leaving \nthe front behind in Iowa.  Moisture return ahead of the front still \nlooks very limited due to the strong ridge locked in place over the \neastern U.S. and extending across the Gulf Coast.  Deterministic \nguidance has virtually zero chance for precip for our area \nassociated with this wave Wednesday night into Thursday due to the \nlack of moisture, and fewer than 20 percent of the LREF members show \nany precip.  The front never gets into Missouri or central Illinois, \nso the warming trend and southerly flow continues through the end of \nthe week, with highs rising well above normal into the upper 60s and \nlow 70s.  The ridge finally weakens ahead of the next short wave \nwhich is forecast to move into the Plains late Friday night into \nSaturday morning.  This wave is stronger than the previous one, and \nit pushes a cold front through Missouri and Illinois \nSaturday/Saturday night.  However, EOF patterns in the LREF show \nvariance in the speed and amplitude of the trough leading to lower \nconfidence in the timing of the front and potential impacts. \nDeterministic guidance continues to show some modest low level \nmoisture convergence ahead of the front leading to some precip \nSaturday and Saturday night.  The NBM's PoPs for Saturday look lower \nthan previous runs this morning, tho they're still in the 30-50 \npercent range for a large part of the forecast area.  The LREF is \nshowing an ~60 percent chance for at least 0.01 inch of rain across \nthe area Saturday into Saturday night, but the probability for at \nleast 0.10 inch is 30 percent or less.  Unfortunately this suggests \nthat any rainfall we receive next weekend will be light and won't do \nmuch (if anything) to alleviate the increasingly dry conditions \nacross the area.  Temperatures will fall behind the cold front on \nSunday although the LREF is showing a decent amount of variance. \nTemperature IQRs increase from only 2-4 degrees earlier in the \nforecast period to 6-10 degrees on Sunday.  However, even the 75th-\n90th percentile temperatures on Sunday are colder than Saturday's \ntemperatures so it should be a notifiable cooldown.",
